  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 121 PM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nTuesday - Wednesday: Showers and Storms Likely \n\nA notable shift in the synoptic pattern occurs as we head into \nTuesday with the upper-level ridging providing our clear skies for \nMonday pushes east and is replaced by a broader troughing regime. As \nthis occurs, a surface boundary will push through the local area, \nwith the recent REFS having very high probabilities (50-100%) for \nmuch of the local area to see measurable rainfall. Currently, the \natmosphere is not overly favorable for severe weather across \nportions of northeast IA and southwest WI where more robust \nconvective development seems more unlikely with very skinny \ninstability profiles and not overly impressive hodographs. \nConsequently, thinking any organized severe convection appears \nunlikely during this timeframe. \n\nHowever, the environment where precipitation forms Tuesday and into \nWednesday appears relatively favorable for efficient rainfall \nproducers across portions of northeast IA and southwest WI where \nprecipitable waters are progged to reach 1.75-2\" and warm cloud \ndepths approach 4km. Consequently, the 03.12z REFS has fairly \nrespectable probabilities (10-50%) for an inch or greater of \nrainfall across portions of northeast IA and southwest WI. In any \ncase, with the frontal passage and precipitation/cloud cover \nlingering around for Wednesday, high temperatures will likely trend \ncooler than normal with highs struggling to reach 80 for most \nlocations. \n\nThursday - Friday: Seasonable, Small Shower and Storm Chances \n\nThursday will likely feature a break in any precipitation chances as \nsurface high pressure builds into the region in the wake of the \naforementioned frontal passage. Additionally, with surface \nsouthwesterly flow pushing into the area, expecting temperatures to \nrebound slightly from Wednesday with highs in the middle 70s to \nlower 80s. As we head into Friday, a troughing regime pushes east \ninto the region, with northwesterly flow bringing some small \nprecipitation chances (10-30%) with the grand ensemble \n(GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble) for Friday. However, with questions on \nwhere any surface forcing would be, confidence is low in any \nconvective development at this point. \n\nThis Weekend: Increasing Warmth with Additional Storm Chances \n\nBy the time the weekend rolls around, zonal to northwesterly flow \nestablishes itself into the region with continued southwesterly \nsurface flow. Consequently, temperatures are expected to steadily \nwarm through the weekend with the grand ensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian \nensemble) inter-quartile spread ranging from the middle 80s to \nmiddle 90s by Sunday for high temperatures across the region. With \nthe zonal to northwesterly flow in place, the key question that will \nneed to be monitored for over the weekend is how far northeast does \nthe ridge situated west of the local area build. Currently, there \nare some respectable probabilities (20-40%) for 1000 J/kg of SBCAPE \nand 30 kts of shear, so there is certainly some potential for severe \nweather when also considering AI guidance (CSU/Pangu/FengWu) does \npaint some probabilities (5-15%) as well. That being said, it will \nbe highly contingent on how quickly the ridge builds in, should it \nprogress into the region faster, this would decrease any convective \npotential. Regardless, thinking the low-end precipitation \nprobabilities (15-40%) in the NBM for this weekend seem appropriate \ngiven the uncertainty of how this weekend may evolve.",
